WEBVTT

00:00.530 --> 00:04.770
Part D. Let p sub 4 of x
be the fourth degree Taylor

00:04.770 --> 00:07.560
polynomial for f
about x equals 0.

00:07.560 --> 00:09.940
Using information
from the graph of y

00:09.940 --> 00:13.700
is equal to the absolute value
of the fifth derivative of f

00:13.700 --> 00:17.750
of x, shown above-- and that's
the graph right over here,

00:17.750 --> 00:19.870
I put it on the side
to save space-- show

00:19.870 --> 00:22.530
that the absolute
value of the difference

00:22.530 --> 00:25.370
between the polynomial--
the fourth degree polynomial

00:25.370 --> 00:28.510
evaluated at 1/4 and the
function evaluated at 1/4

00:28.510 --> 00:32.060
is less than one over 3,000.

00:32.060 --> 00:35.430
So this is, it seems like quite
an interesting problem here.

00:35.430 --> 00:37.676
So first, let's just think
about what they're asking.

00:37.676 --> 00:39.050
They're asking
for the difference

00:39.050 --> 00:42.120
between the polynomial and
the function evaluated 1/4.

00:42.120 --> 00:43.780
In other words,
they're asking us

00:43.780 --> 00:48.660
to-- they're asking us to
bound the error of the function

00:48.660 --> 00:53.494
approximation at
x is equal to 1/4.

00:53.494 --> 00:54.910
So let me draw a
little graph here

00:54.910 --> 00:56.743
just so we can visualize
it a little better.

00:56.743 --> 01:00.560
So if that's our y-axis and--
let me draw it like this.

01:00.560 --> 01:05.230
So this is our y-axis
and this is our x-axis.

01:05.230 --> 01:07.440
And our function, let's
say our function looks

01:07.440 --> 01:08.610
something like this.

01:08.610 --> 01:11.310
Our polynomial is centered at 0.

01:11.310 --> 01:14.320
Our fourth degree
polynomial is centered at 0.

01:14.320 --> 01:17.140
And so it'll be equal
to the function at 0.

01:17.140 --> 01:20.570
And then it'll become
probably a worse approximation

01:20.570 --> 01:23.436
as we get further and
further away from 0.

01:23.436 --> 01:24.810
And what they're
saying is, let's

01:24.810 --> 01:26.950
bound how bad of
an approximation

01:26.950 --> 01:29.540
that is when x is equal to 1/4.

01:29.540 --> 01:34.010
So if we say that this is x is
equal to 1/4 right over here,

01:34.010 --> 01:38.030
they're saying, look, take the
value of the polynomial at 1/4,

01:38.030 --> 01:41.192
subtract from that the value
of the function at 1/4,

01:41.192 --> 01:42.900
and then take the
absolute value and then

01:42.900 --> 01:46.160
that'll essentially give us the
absolute value of the error.

01:46.160 --> 01:49.420
Or that will give us the
absolute value of the remainder

01:49.420 --> 01:52.120
or give us the distance between
the two functions there.

01:52.120 --> 01:56.860
And they want us to
bound that distance.

01:56.860 --> 01:59.330
Now, to do this problem
there is something

01:59.330 --> 02:00.930
that you just kind
of have to know.

02:00.930 --> 02:02.940
And I've proven it
in another series

02:02.940 --> 02:04.960
of videos-- two videos
I think it took me--

02:04.960 --> 02:07.210
in the Khan Academy
Calculus playlist.

02:07.210 --> 02:11.120
If you do a search for bounded
or error or approximation

02:11.120 --> 02:13.830
or Taylor, you should
probably find it.

02:13.830 --> 02:20.130
And that there's a general idea
here which we've proved that

02:20.130 --> 02:22.910
if you have some function-- so
I'm going to show the general--

02:22.910 --> 02:28.650
so if you have some function
f of x and that you have some

02:28.650 --> 02:32.230
polynomial approximation
of it of degree n---

02:32.230 --> 02:35.230
some polynomial
approximation of degree n.

02:35.230 --> 02:37.620
And let's say that this
polynomial approximation is

02:37.620 --> 02:38.515
centered at a.

02:38.515 --> 02:41.320
In our particular case, a is 0.

02:41.320 --> 02:43.670
So that you're centered at a.

02:43.670 --> 02:48.170
So we could write-- we
could say this is about a.

02:48.170 --> 02:52.010
It's an approximation
about x equals a.

02:52.010 --> 02:53.850
We can bound the error.

02:53.850 --> 02:56.834
So let me first define
this error function here.

02:56.834 --> 02:58.250
Sometimes it's
called a remainder.

02:58.250 --> 03:01.710
So we could say, the error
function for the nth degree

03:01.710 --> 03:05.520
polynomial about a-- the
n-th degree Taylor polynomial

03:05.520 --> 03:08.750
approximation about a-- it
will be a function of x.

03:08.750 --> 03:12.490
So for any other x that you
pick, what will this error be?

03:12.490 --> 03:15.600
Is equal to the difference
between these two things.

03:15.600 --> 03:19.070
You could say it's f of x minus
that or that minus f of x.

03:19.070 --> 03:20.790
But let me just
write it this way.

03:20.790 --> 03:27.060
This is equal to f of x minus
the polynomial approximation

03:27.060 --> 03:27.896
of x.

03:27.896 --> 03:30.020
And if you want to take
the absolute value of this,

03:30.020 --> 03:31.430
that's equal to
absolute value of that.

03:31.430 --> 03:32.860
And if you're taking
absolute values,

03:32.860 --> 03:34.110
then you can switch the order.

03:34.110 --> 03:36.400
So this is the same thing
as the absolute value

03:36.400 --> 03:41.870
of the polynomial approximation
at x minus f of x.

03:41.870 --> 03:47.060
So the thing that you just need
to know is, we can bound this.

03:47.060 --> 03:51.050
We can bound this if
we know some properties

03:51.050 --> 03:52.740
about the n plus 1.

03:52.740 --> 03:54.600
So this is the nth
degree approximation.

03:54.600 --> 03:58.930
If we know some properties about
the n plus 1th derivative of f.

03:58.930 --> 04:07.830
So if we know that
the n plus 1th is

04:07.830 --> 04:15.060
the derivative of f is less
than or equal to some maximum

04:15.060 --> 04:16.990
value-- and in particular,
the absolute value

04:16.990 --> 04:19.570
of the n plus 1th
derivative of f--

04:19.570 --> 04:22.470
is less than or equal to
some-- we could call this

04:22.470 --> 04:29.200
some maximum value--
over the interval, over x

04:29.200 --> 04:33.910
is in the interval
between a and some b,

04:33.910 --> 04:35.530
where b is greater than a.

04:40.890 --> 04:45.919
Then we can make the statement--
and this is, once again,

04:45.919 --> 04:47.460
something that I
prove in that video.

04:47.460 --> 04:49.376
I think I call it the
boundedness of the error

04:49.376 --> 04:51.020
remainder for Taylor
approximation.

04:51.020 --> 04:52.260
Something like that.

04:52.260 --> 04:59.460
Then we know that the
error function at any given

04:59.460 --> 05:04.180
x where x is greater than or--
let me say this in particular.

05:04.180 --> 05:08.070
Is any given x that's part
of this interval-- where

05:08.070 --> 05:10.110
x is part of this
interval between a

05:10.110 --> 05:17.310
and b-- is going to be less
than or equal to M times

05:17.310 --> 05:23.950
x minus a to the n plus
1th power over n plus 1.

05:23.950 --> 05:25.450
So this is the thing
that you really

05:25.450 --> 05:29.630
have to know ahead of time to
be able to do this problem.

05:29.630 --> 05:32.610
It would be nearly impossible in
the time constraints of the AP

05:32.610 --> 05:35.440
exam to prove this
from first principles.

05:35.440 --> 05:36.990
I encourage you to
watch that video

05:36.990 --> 05:39.370
so you can understand
how this proof happens

05:39.370 --> 05:40.520
from first principles.

05:40.520 --> 05:44.020
But going into the AP
exam, based on the facts

05:44.020 --> 05:46.610
that this did show
up in the 2011 exam,

05:46.610 --> 05:48.640
it's probably good to
know this property,

05:48.640 --> 05:49.772
to know how to bound it.

05:49.772 --> 05:52.230
And this is clearly what they're
asking for because they're

05:52.230 --> 05:56.400
giving us-- so we know
what the fourth-- we

05:56.400 --> 05:58.180
know what p sub four is.

05:58.180 --> 06:00.480
p sub 4 of x-- it's up
to the fourth degree

06:00.480 --> 06:03.480
term of the polynomial
approximation of f about 0.

06:03.480 --> 06:05.750
In part B, we got all the
way the sixth degree term.

06:05.750 --> 06:07.930
So if you want to go just
to the fourth degree term,

06:07.930 --> 06:09.840
this is what p sub 4 is.

06:09.840 --> 06:16.720
So this is 1 plus x squared over
2 plus x to the fourth over 4.

06:16.720 --> 06:18.180
So we know that.

06:18.180 --> 06:24.080
And we want to bound this
thing right over here.

06:24.080 --> 06:28.350
So if we can find an M-- so
this is the fourth degree

06:28.350 --> 06:31.100
polynomial.

06:31.100 --> 06:32.850
This is the fourth
degree error that we're

06:32.850 --> 06:34.860
talking about in this case.

06:34.860 --> 06:36.100
Let me write it this way.

06:36.100 --> 06:38.096
This is the nth error
in the general case.

06:38.096 --> 06:39.970
But we want to bound
the fourth degree error.

06:39.970 --> 06:42.490
So if we want bound the
fourth degree error--

06:42.490 --> 06:47.794
so we can say the fourth
degree error-- at b.

06:47.794 --> 06:49.210
In this case, we're
going to say--

06:49.210 --> 06:51.330
or at 1/4 I should say, not b.

06:51.330 --> 06:52.670
I never mentioned a b before.

06:52.670 --> 06:54.450
I mentioned the b
when I prove it.

06:54.450 --> 06:56.990
The fourth degree
error at 1/4 is

06:56.990 --> 06:59.850
going to be less than or equal
to-- I did mention a b here

06:59.850 --> 07:03.020
so if you take this
to be 1/4, and 1/4

07:03.020 --> 07:05.550
is part of that interval
so you can apply it here.

07:05.550 --> 07:08.720
So the fourth degree error,
I guess you could say,

07:08.720 --> 07:13.620
at 1/4 is going to be less
than or equal to some M times

07:13.620 --> 07:18.730
b-- I should say, 1/4 minus a.

07:18.730 --> 07:22.430
a in our particular case
for this problem is a 0.

07:22.430 --> 07:28.100
So 1/4 minus is just 0
to the n plus 1th power.

07:28.100 --> 07:31.770
So this is 4-- this is the
fourth degree polynomial. n is

07:31.770 --> 07:34.740
4 so the n plus 1 power
is the fifth power.

07:34.740 --> 07:37.430
All of that over--
and sorry, this

07:37.430 --> 07:39.510
was supposed to be n
plus 1-- all of that

07:39.510 --> 07:41.090
over n plus 1 factorial.

07:41.090 --> 07:44.737
So this is going to
be over 5 factorial.

07:44.737 --> 07:45.820
Don't want to confuse you.

07:45.820 --> 07:50.450
This was-- all of that is
over n plus 1 factorial.

07:50.450 --> 07:53.560
So all of this is
over 5 factorial.

07:53.560 --> 08:05.140
So we can make this statement
if we know that the fifth

08:05.140 --> 08:07.200
derivative-- the n
plus 1th derivative--

08:07.200 --> 08:11.150
if we know that the fifth
derivative of f is less than

08:11.150 --> 08:14.750
or equal to some value M-- the
absolute value of the fifth

08:14.750 --> 08:17.720
derivative of f, I should say---
is less than or equal to some

08:17.720 --> 08:29.810
value at M over the interval
x is between 0 and 1/4.

08:29.810 --> 08:32.419
Those are our a's and b's
in this particular case.

08:32.419 --> 08:34.169
So when you look at
the graph-- and that's

08:34.169 --> 08:35.252
exactly what they gave us.

08:35.252 --> 08:37.590
They gave us the graph
of the absolute value

08:37.590 --> 08:39.409
of the fifth derivative of x.

08:39.409 --> 08:42.270
And 1/4 is sitting
right over here.

08:42.270 --> 08:43.669
So this is 1/4.

08:43.669 --> 08:46.510
We don't know exactly
what we hit over here.

08:46.510 --> 08:50.070
This looks like it's roughly,
I don't know, 31 or 32.

08:50.070 --> 08:54.330
But we know it's
definitely less than 40

08:54.330 --> 08:57.200
over the interval
between 0 and 1/4.

08:57.200 --> 08:59.125
So we can pick M to be 40.

09:04.100 --> 09:06.950
We know that this is
less than or equal to 40

09:06.950 --> 09:08.280
over that interval.

09:08.280 --> 09:10.140
So we can make this statement.

09:10.140 --> 09:12.340
And so we know that this
thing right over here

09:12.340 --> 09:14.400
is going to be less
because this over here

09:14.400 --> 09:16.080
is the same as this over here.

09:16.080 --> 09:20.000
We know this is less than--
let me write it all out.

09:20.000 --> 09:23.421
This is-- and I know of people
who got fives on this example

09:23.421 --> 09:24.670
without being able to do this.

09:24.670 --> 09:26.920
So don't stress out if this
seems really bizarre too.

09:26.920 --> 09:28.970
This is really so
they can separate out

09:28.970 --> 09:31.110
who happened to
know this, really.

09:31.110 --> 09:38.930
So this we can say is
based on this property,

09:38.930 --> 09:43.850
we can say is less than
or equal to 40 times 1/4

09:43.850 --> 09:45.560
to the fifth power.

09:45.560 --> 09:48.320
So this is 40 times
1/4 to the fifth power.

09:48.320 --> 09:51.360
So I could just say divided
by 4 to the fifth power

09:51.360 --> 09:53.400
times-- and then the
denominator over here--

09:53.400 --> 09:54.250
we have 5 factorial.

09:54.250 --> 09:55.208
I'll just write it out.

09:55.208 --> 09:57.416
5 times 4 times 3 times 2.

09:57.416 --> 09:58.790
And we don't have
to write the 1.

09:58.790 --> 10:00.430
That doesn't change the value.

10:00.430 --> 10:02.140
And now we can simplify this.

10:02.140 --> 10:04.980
We can divide the numerator
and the denominator by 4.

10:04.980 --> 10:07.710
So this becomes 10,
that just becomes a 1.

10:07.710 --> 10:09.120
We can divide it by 5.

10:09.120 --> 10:11.560
This becomes a 2,
that becomes a 1.

10:11.560 --> 10:13.180
We can divide by 1.

10:13.180 --> 10:15.840
That becomes a-- oh,
sorry, we can divide by 2.

10:15.840 --> 10:18.090
That becomes a 1,
that becomes a 1.

10:18.090 --> 10:20.400
And you say that this
quantity over here

10:20.400 --> 10:23.610
is going to be equal to 1.

10:23.610 --> 10:25.200
So let me write it.

10:25.200 --> 10:30.130
So all of this stuff is less
than or equal to 1 over 4

10:30.130 --> 10:35.080
to the fifth times 3.

10:38.390 --> 10:40.680
That's all we have left
in the denominator.

10:40.680 --> 10:42.131
And so what is 4 to the fifth?

10:42.131 --> 10:43.130
You may or may not know.

10:43.130 --> 10:45.660
You can work it out by hand if
you like, but 4 to the fifth

10:45.660 --> 10:47.840
is the same thing as
2 to the 10th power

10:47.840 --> 10:49.457
because 4 is 2 squared.

10:49.457 --> 10:52.040
2 to the 10th power, if you're
familiar with computer science,

10:52.040 --> 10:52.900
is 1,024.

10:52.900 --> 10:54.660
But you can work
it out yourself.

10:54.660 --> 10:57.050
So this is going to
be-- this is less than

10:57.050 --> 11:05.770
or equal to 1,024 times 3.

11:05.770 --> 11:06.640
So what is that?

11:06.640 --> 11:13.020
That is less than or
equal to 1 over 3.

11:13.020 --> 11:14.113
Oh, what is it?

11:14.113 --> 11:15.350
72?

11:15.350 --> 11:16.691
3,000.

11:16.691 --> 11:19.570
1 over 3,072.

11:19.570 --> 11:24.050
So this quantity right over
here is less than 1 over 3,072.

11:24.050 --> 11:24.930
We know.

11:24.930 --> 11:26.540
We know that for a fact.

11:26.540 --> 11:29.830
Well, if something is
less than 1 over 3,072,

11:29.830 --> 11:32.300
it's definitely going to
be less than 1 over 3,000.

11:32.300 --> 11:35.102
1 over 3,000 is a larger number.

11:35.102 --> 11:36.310
It has a smaller denominator.

11:36.310 --> 11:41.380
That's definitely less than
or equal to 1 over 3,000.

11:41.380 --> 11:43.500
And then we are done.

11:43.500 --> 11:46.400
We've proven that this is
less than 1 over 3,000.

11:46.400 --> 11:51.860
We've proven that the error at
1/4 is less than 1 over 3,000.
